# Build context hygiene for the production image.
#
# Railway builds from a git checkout, so it never sees untracked files; a local
# `docker build .` does. Without this file the context is ~4 GB instead of ~350 kB,
# because the local `.build/` directory alone is 4 GB. A build context that large is
# slow to send and, worse, `COPY . .` would drop a stale host-built `.build/` on top
# of the image and let the release build reuse artefacts that were never produced by
# this image's toolchain.

# Local build products. Never belong in the image: the release build inside the
# builder stage produces its own.
.build/

# Version control metadata. Not needed to compile.
.git/
.gitignore

# Untracked working material that is not part of the server build.
docs/
PayloadStorage/
.DS_Store

# Tracked test media. Neither the server sources nor the test sources reference
# these two files; they are repository artefacts, verified unreferenced before
# being excluded here.
real-image.icns
real-render-test.jpg

# The build recipe itself does not need to be inside the image.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# NOTE ON Tests/ — deliberately NOT excluded.
#
# Excluding Tests/ breaks the build. Package.swift declares the test target with an
# implicit path; when Tests/ is absent SwiftPM falls back to inferring that target's
# sources from the package root, which then overlaps the library and runner targets:
#
#   error: target 'AIHOSAssetServerTests' has overlapping sources: ...
#
# Verified empirically against this commit. Keeping Tests/ in the *build context* is
# therefore required. It costs 164 kB and it does not reach the runtime image, because
# the runtime stage copies the built binary alone and never the source tree.
